          kind: "translation",
          refs: ['g3.36.2', 'g3.36.11'],
          lemma: "πιστεύων … ἀπειθῶν",
          title: "The opposite of “believe” is “disobey”",
          body: "The chapter’s last verse sets **πιστεύων**, *“believing,”* against **ἀπειθῶν** — not “not believing” but **“disobeying, refusing to be persuaded.”** For John, faith is not bare opinion but a *yielding* of the whole self to the Son; unbelief is a *refusal*. That is why the stakes are so high and so present-tense: the one who trusts the Son **has** (already) eternal life, while over the one who will not, the **wrath of God** — his settled opposition to evil — *remains*, as it did before the light came. The verdict of v. 18 is simply restated: it turns on what you do with the Son.",
